{
  "version": 1,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "c_t40001",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Client cells and the bridge",
        "",
        "Draw in the page with client cells, and call from them the functions a server cell exports.",
        "",
        "You will draw HTML from a cell, call a server function from the page, push live updates from the server to the page, and see what can cross between the two and what cannot. About 10 minutes.",
        "",
        "So far every cell ran on the server, in Node. A **client cell** runs here, in this browser tab, with `document`, `window` and every browser API. Its header says **client**. Click **Run All** to start; the last code cell fails on purpose."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40002",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 1. Draw in the page",
        "",
        "`display.div()` gives the cell a container under its code. Whatever you put in it shows there."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40003",
      "type": "client",
      "source": [
        "const box = display.div();",
        "box.innerHTML = `<p style=\"font: 600 1.25rem sans-serif; margin: 0\">Hello from the page</p>`;",
        "box.querySelector(\"p\")!.style.color = \"tomato\";",
        "navigator.language"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40004",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The cell drew its HTML, and its last value, `navigator.language`, is your browser's language: this code ran in the page, not in Node.",
        "",
        "Run it again: the container is emptied first, so you never get two copies. To add a client cell yourself, press **Escape** then **Shift+C**, or click **+ Client**.",
        "",
        "Client cells run in the tab you have open. Reload the page and what they drew is gone until you run them again; the server cells keep their state in the kernel."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40005",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 2. Call the server from the page",
        "",
        "A server cell that writes `export` in front of a function makes it callable from client cells."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40006",
      "type": "server",
      "source": [
        "const stock = new Map([",
        "  [\"apples\", 12],",
        "  [\"pears\", 3],",
        "  [\"plums\", 0],",
        "]);",
        "",
        "export function inStock(fruit: string): number {",
        "  return stock.get(fruit) ?? 0;",
        "}",
        "",
        "export function restock(fruit: string, count: number) {",
        "  stock.set(fruit, inStock(fruit) + count);",
        "  console.log(`restocked ${count} ${fruit}`);",
        "  return Object.fromEntries(stock);",
        "}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40007",
      "type": "client",
      "source": [
        "const pears = await inStock(\"pears\");",
        "display.div().textContent = `${pears} pears in stock`;",
        "pears"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40008",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`inStock` ran in Node and its answer came back to the page. Seen from a client cell, an exported function is always **async**: call it with `await`. Hover `inStock` in the client cell: its type is `(fruit: string) => Promise<number>`, taken from the server cell.",
        "",
        "Open the **Inspector** on the right (**Cmd+Alt+B** if it is hidden): its **Bridge** section lists what the client cells use from the server (functions, values and events), each with its type, the cells that use it and its last call."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40009",
      "type": "client",
      "source": [
        "const box = display.div();",
        "const button = document.createElement(\"button\");",
        "const answer = document.createElement(\"pre\");",
        "button.textContent = \"Restock 5 plums\";",
        "button.addEventListener(\"click\", async () => {",
        "  answer.textContent = JSON.stringify(await restock(\"plums\", 5));",
        "});",
        "box.append(button, answer);"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40010",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Click the button a few times. Each click calls `restock` on the server: the page shows the new stock, and the server cell that defines `restock` shows its `console.log` lines, since that is where the function runs. Secrets in `.env` stay on the server: the page gets only what the function returns."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40011",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 3. Live updates from the server",
        "",
        "A server cell can push events to the page with `emit(name, data)`; a client cell listens with `on(name, callback)`. The client cell below listens; the server cell after it sends four steps, one every 0.6 seconds. Run the client cell first, then the server cell, and watch the bar."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40012",
      "type": "client",
      "source": [
        "const bar = document.createElement(\"progress\");",
        "bar.max = 100;",
        "bar.value = 0;",
        "const step = document.createElement(\"span\");",
        "display.div().append(bar, step);",
        "",
        "on(\"progress\", (data: { done: number; step: string }) => {",
        "  bar.value = data.done;",
        "  step.textContent = ` ${data.step}`;",
        "});"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40013",
      "type": "server",
      "source": [
        "const steps = [",
        "  [25, \"washing\"],",
        "  [50, \"peeling\"],",
        "  [75, \"slicing\"],",
        "  [100, \"done\"],",
        "] as const;",
        "",
        "for (const [done, step] of steps) {",
        "  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 600));",
        "  emit(\"progress\", { done, step });",
        "}",
        "`sent ${steps.length} events`"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40014",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 4. What crosses, and what cannot",
        "",
        "Arguments and results cross as **plain data**: numbers, strings, booleans, arrays, plain objects, `Date`, `Map`, `Set` and bytes. An exported value that is plain data arrives in the client cell as a **copy**: changing it in the page does not change it on the server.",
        "",
        "Anything else does not cross: a class instance keeps its methods on the server, and a copy without them would break later, far from the cause. The run fails at once instead, with an error that names the variable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40015",
      "type": "server",
      "source": [
        "class Basket {",
        "  items: string[] = [];",
        "  add(item: string) {",
        "    this.items.push(item);",
        "  }",
        "}",
        "",
        "export const prices = { apples: 0.5, pears: 0.8, plums: 1.2 };",
        "export const updatedAt = new Date();",
        "export const basket = new Basket();"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40016",
      "type": "client",
      "source": [
        "({ prices, updatedAt, isDate: updatedAt instanceof Date })"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40017",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The next cell reads `basket` and fails **on purpose**: read its error. The way out is the one it suggests: export a function that uses the basket, and call that function from the page."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40018",
      "type": "client",
      "source": [
        "basket.items"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c_t40019",
      "type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Try it",
        "",
        "1. Export `function basketItems() { return basket.items; }` from a new server cell, run it, and call `await basketItems()` from a client cell.",
        "2. Make the restock button read a number from an `<input>` instead of always adding 5.",
        "3. In the **Inspector**, switch **Variables** to **Client**: which names did the client cells define?",
        "",
        "Next: `05-packages-and-secrets`, npm packages in both kinds of cells, and secrets kept on the server."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
